Make posix_spawn fork instead of clone, for a kernel with no shared address space.

musl reaches the child of posix_spawn, and therefore of system(3) and popen(3),
through __clone with CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK: the child runs in the PARENT's
address space on a stack of its own until it execs. Rach2 has no way to share an
address space between two processes, and a syscall that accepted those flags and
quietly handed back a private copy-on-write image would be reporting semantics it
does not provide. So syscall 120 stays unimplemented here, and this patch makes
posix_spawn use the fork this kernel does have.

The child function never returns (it execs, or writes its errno to the pipe and
_exits), and it communicates failure through that pipe rather than through shared
memory, so a forked child serves it exactly as a cloned one did. The raw syscall
is used rather than fork(3) to keep the negative-errno return the error path
below already expects, and to leave the atfork handlers out of a call that is not
a fork as far as the caller is concerned.

--- a/src/process/posix_spawn.c
+++ b/src/process/posix_spawn.c
@@ -195,8 +195,14 @@
 		goto fail;
 	}
 
-	pid = __clone(child, stack+sizeof stack,
-		CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK|SIGCHLD, &args);
+	/* Rach2: this kernel has no clone and no shared address space, so the
+	 * child is forked and runs the same function on its own copy. `child`
+	 * never returns: it execs, or reports its errno through the pipe and
+	 * exits. The raw syscall keeps the negative-errno convention the error
+	 * path below expects. */
+	(void)stack;
+	pid = __syscall(SYS_fork);
+	if (!pid) child(&args);
 	close(args.p[1]);
 	UNLOCK(__abort_lock);
 
